    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one most people should buy: dual fuel, light, quiet enough, and a fraction of the Honda's price.",
    "description": "Champion's 201323 2500-watt dual fuel inverter is the practical pick in this class. It is rated at 2,500 starting watts on both fuels, 1,850 running watts on gasoline and 1,665 on propane, and at a marketed 39 pounds it is one of the lightest 2,000-watt-class generators sold. The propane option is the reason to choose it over a gas-only rival: a 20-pound cylinder stores indefinitely without stabilizer, and Champion rates it for up to 34 hours at quarter load, against 11.5 hours from the 1.1-gallon gas tank. CO Shield shuts the engine down automatically if carbon monoxide builds up around it. Champion lists the noise at 53 dBA at 23 feet, a few decibels behind the Honda at light load and not a difference most neighbors will notice. Outlets are a 20-amp 120V duplex, a 12V DC outlet and USB; there is no TT-30R on the unit, so RV owners need an adapter or the parallel kit. It is parallel ready with a second Champion 2500, and the kit adds a 30-amp RV outlet and about 3,700 running watts. Long-term build quality does not match the Honda, but for the money nothing in this class does more."
  },
  {
    "id": "wen-56235i",
    "name": "WEN 56235i Super Quiet 2350-Watt Portable Inverter Generator",
    "category": "Budget Inverter, 2,350W Surge / 1,900W Running",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "1,900 rated watts, 100 more than the Honda and Yamaha",
      "About 39 lb with a 1.06-gallon tank",
      "WEN markets it at about 51 dBA at quarter load",
