    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way into a real inverter in this class. Fine for camping and short outages; skip it if you want a CO sensor.",
    "description": "The WEN 56235i is the budget answer, and on paper it gives away less than the price suggests. The manual rates it at 2,350 surge watts and 1,900 running watts from a 79cc engine, 100 running watts more than the Honda EU2200i, in a 39-pound package with a 1.06-gallon tank. WEN markets it at roughly 51 dBA at quarter load, which puts it in the same band as the premium units at light load. Outlets are two 120V 5-20R receptacles, a 12V DC outlet and a USB port, and the manual warns not to exceed 15.8 amps on the household outlets when running a single unit. The manual lists 5.7 hours at half load, so a whole night of fridge and fan duty means a refill or a second tank. The fuel shut-off is a genuinely useful detail: run the carburetor dry before storage and you avoid the gummed jets that kill most cheap generators. It parallels with a second WEN inverter through a kit sold separately. What you give up is the CO shut-off sensor several rivals now include, the engine pedigree and the resale value of the Honda."
  },
