    "description": "Yamaha's EF2200iS is the other premium nameplate in this class, and it makes a different trade than the Honda. Output is the same, 1,800 running and 2,200 peak watts, but it carries a larger 1.24-gallon tank and Yamaha rates it for 10.5 hours at quarter load, two and a half hours more than the EU2200i. The price of that tank is weight and noise. It weighs 55.2 pounds dry, about eight pounds more than the Honda and sixteen more than the Champion, and Yamaha lists noise at 57 to 65 dBA from quarter load to rated load. Against Honda's 48 to 57 dB(A), that is a real gap: nine decibels at light load is close to half as loud again. For home backup in a yard or a work truck, where runtime between refills is worth more than a few decibels, it makes sense, and Yamaha's dealer support is comparable to Honda's. For quiet campgrounds or anyone who carries the generator any distance, the Honda or the Champion is the better choice."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best 2000 Watt Inverter Generators",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-2000-watt-inverter-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 2000 watt inverter generator run a refrigerator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. A typical full-size refrigerator runs on 100 to 250 watts and needs about 1,000 to 1,500 watts for a moment when the compressor starts. Every generator in this class starts and runs one easily, and most can carry a chest freezer, lights, a router and phone chargers at the same time, staying under 1,000 running watts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a 2000 watt generator run a microwave?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, on its own. A 1,000-watt microwave typically draws 1,400 to 1,600 watts from the outlet, which fits under an 1,800-watt running rating. Run it at the same time as a refrigerator starting up and you may trip the overload. The Generac GP2500i, at 2,200 running watts, gives the most margin for both."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a 2000 watt generator run an RV air conditioner?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally not on a single unit. A 13,500 BTU rooftop AC typically needs 2,500 to 3,500 watts to start. Two 2,000-watt-class units in parallel usually start it, and a soft-start kit on the air conditioner lowers the surge enough that some single units cope. A small 5,000 BTU window unit is usually fine on one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a 2000 watt inverter generator run on a tank?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturers list about 8 to 11.5 hours at quarter load from a one-gallon tank, and 3 to 7 hours at half to full load. Honda rates the EU2200i at 8.1 hours at quarter load; Westinghouse rates the iGen2550 at 11. On propane, Champion rates the 201323 at up to 34 hours from a 20-pound tank."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the Honda EU2200i worth the extra money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you'll use it for years, possibly. It has the lowest published noise figures in the class, a commercial-series engine and strong resale value. It doesn't give you more watts than a Champion or WEN costing half as much. If output per dollar is the priority, the Champion is the better buy; if quiet and longevity matter, the Honda earns its premium."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does parallel capable mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Two identical or compatible inverter generators can be linked with a parallel kit so their output combines into one set of outlets. Two 1,800-watt units give about 3,600 running watts. It lets you carry two 40-pound units instead of one 100-pound unit, and run only one when you need less power. Brands generally can't be mixed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I hook a 2000 watt generator to my house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not through a standard transfer switch. None of the units here has a 240V outlet, so they cannot feed both legs of a home's panel or power 240V appliances. Use outdoor-rated extension cords to the appliances you need. If you want to power wired circuits, look at a 3,500-watt or larger generator with a 30-amp outlet and a transfer switch."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
