        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A 2,000-watt inverter generator will keep a refrigerator, lights and the internet running, power a small trailer, and sit quietly at a campsite. It will not run central air, a well pump or a space heater alongside anything else. Buy one for what it does well and pair two when you need more.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-the-class-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a 2,000-Watt Inverter Generator Actually Is
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Class in Numbers</h3>

          <p>
            The "2,000-watt" label covers a tight band of machines: 2,000 to 2,550 peak watts, 1,600 to 2,200 running watts, an 80cc to 125cc engine, a tank of about one gallon, and a weight between 39 and 55 pounds. The headline number on the box is almost always the peak or starting figure. The number you plan around is running watts, and in this class most models sit at 1,800 to 1,900. A generator sold as 2,500 watts usually runs at 1,850, and one sold as 2,200 usually runs at 1,800.
          </p>

          <p>
            That spread matters more than it looks. The difference between 1,800 and 2,200 running watts is a microwave. It is also why we treat everything from the Honda EU2200i to the Champion 2500 as one class: they do the same jobs and fail at the same jobs. If you need a broader view across sizes, our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">inverter generator guide</a> covers units up to 7,000 watts. This page is only about the small ones and what they can realistically power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Inverter Output Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Every unit here is an inverter. The engine drives an alternator that produces raw AC, which is converted to DC and then rebuilt into a clean 120V sine wave. That gives you low harmonic distortion, safe for laptops, TVs, CPAP machines and the control boards in modern refrigerators and furnaces. It also lets the engine throttle down when the load is light, which is where the quiet operation and long quarter-load runtimes come from. A conventional open-frame generator of the same wattage runs flat out regardless of load. If you are deciding between the two types, our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator comparison</a> sets out the trade.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who Should and Should Not Buy One</h3>

          <p>
            Who should buy one? Campers and tailgaters, anyone with a small travel trailer, and homeowners who want to keep a refrigerator, a freezer, lights, a router and phone chargers going through a short outage. Who should not: anyone counting on running a sump pump, a well pump, central air, an electric range or an electric water heater. Those jobs need the 3,500-watt class or larger, covered in our <a href="/best-3500-watt-generator/">3,500-watt generator guide</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Class at a Glance</p>
