        <div id="what-it-runs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What 1,800 Running Watts Will and Won't Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sizing a small generator comes down to two numbers per appliance: running watts, which it draws continuously, and starting watts, the brief surge a motor or compressor pulls on start-up. A generator has to cover the total running load plus the single largest starting surge at any moment. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical figures for about a hundred appliances; here is how they land against an 1,800-watt running and 2,200-watt peak budget.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Yes: a refrigerator, with room left over</h3>

          <p>
            A modern full-size refrigerator typically runs on 100 to 250 watts and needs roughly 1,000 to 1,500 watts for a second when the compressor starts. Every generator on this list starts and runs one comfortably. Add a chest freezer, LED lighting, a router, a TV and phone chargers and you are still usually under 1,000 running watts. That is the core job for this class, and it does it well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maybe: a microwave</h3>

          <p>
            A microwave's label shows its cooking power, not what it draws. A 1,000-watt microwave commonly pulls 1,400 to 1,600 watts from the outlet. On an 1,800-watt generator that works on its own, but switch it on while the refrigerator compressor is starting and you can trip the overload. The practical rule is to run it alone, or step up to the Generac GP2500i, whose 2,200 running watts give you the margin to run both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Only on its own: a space heater, hair dryer or coffee maker</h3>

          <p>
            Resistive heaters are the trap. A space heater on high draws 1,500 watts continuously, which is 83% of an 1,800-watt generator's continuous rating. It will run, but it will run alone, and the engine will spend the whole time near full throttle at its loudest and thirstiest. Honda rates the EU2200i at 3.2 hours per tank at full load, so a heater on high will empty it in an afternoon. Use the low setting, around 750 watts, and you get the fridge back.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No: central air, and most window units over 8,000 BTU</h3>

          <p>
            A central air conditioner needs 3,000 to 5,000 starting watts. A small 5,000 to 6,000 BTU window unit can run on some 2,000-watt-class inverters, and a 13,500 BTU RV rooftop unit generally cannot start on a single unit without a soft-start kit. If cooling is the point, plan on two units in parallel or a bigger generator. Sump and well pumps are a no for the same reason: their starting surge is two to three times their running draw.
          </p>

