        <div id="parallel-kits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Parallel Kits: Doubling Up Instead of Buying Bigger
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Parallel Kits Work</h3>

          <p>
            Almost every 2,000-watt-class inverter is parallel capable, and that is the most underrated reason to buy one. Connect two identical units with a parallel cable or clip-on kit and their outputs combine into one set of outlets, often including a 30-amp RV receptacle on the kit. Two 1,800-watt units give you 3,600 running watts; two Champion 2500s give roughly 3,700.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Weight Argument</h3>

          <p>
            The case for doing this rather than buying a single 4,000-watt inverter is weight. A 4,000 to 4,500-watt inverter usually weighs 90 to 105 pounds and has to be rolled. Two 2,000-watt units weigh 40 to 47 pounds each, so one person can lift either into a truck bed or carry it to a campsite. On a mild weekend you run one; when you need the air conditioner, you bring the second. You also get redundancy: if one fails to start, you still have half your power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pairing Rules</h3>

          <p>
            There are rules. Pair the same model, or models the manufacturer explicitly lists as compatible. Honda pairs the EU2200i with other compatible Honda inverters; Westinghouse lets its iGen inverters pair with each other and includes the cord with the iGen2550; Champion and WEN sell their own kits. Mixing brands is not supported and can damage both inverters. Also check that the kit has the outlet you need: some kits are household outlets only, others add the TT-30R.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Doubling Up Costs</h3>

          <p>
            The cost is real. Two premium units cost more than one mid-size inverter, and you double your oil changes and spark plugs. If you know you will always need 3,500 watts, a single machine is cheaper. If you need 1,800 watts most of the time and 3,600 only occasionally, buying one now and a second later is the cheapest path to both.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Same model, manufacturer's kit, and don't expect a full doubling of starting watts.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Two units share the starting surge, so most pairs will start a 13,500 BTU RV air conditioner. A soft-start kit on the AC makes it far more reliable.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="top-picks-intro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Chose These Generators
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spec Sheets, Not Bench Tests</h3>

          <p>
            We did not bench test these six generators. Every figure on this page comes from the manufacturer's spec sheet, the owner's manual, or a retailer listing that matches them, and where a manufacturer does not publish a number we say so rather than fill the gap. Ratings and review counts reflect retailer averages at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What We Weighed</h3>

          <p>
            We started with every currently sold gasoline or dual fuel inverter rated between 2,000 and 2,550 peak watts, and cut anything we could not confirm was still in production. We then weighed five things in order: running watts, because that is what actually runs your appliances; published noise, and whether the manufacturer says at what load it was measured; runtime per tank at quarter and half load; weight; and safety features, specifically automatic carbon monoxide shut-off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Spec Sheets Leave Out</h3>

          <p>
            We gave weight to what each spec sheet leaves out. A noise figure without a load is less useful than one with it, and a runtime figure at quarter load says little about running a microwave. Where two models were close we favored the one with the better-documented spec. We left out battery power stations, which our <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">power station vs generator guide</a> covers separately, and open-frame conventional generators, which are louder and have dirtier power.
          </p>
        </div>

