        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise: Reading the dBA Ratings in This Class
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Comparing the Published Figures</h3>

          <p>
            Quiet is the main reason people buy a small inverter, and the numbers here look close: 48, 51, 52, 53, 57 dBA. They are closer than they are comparable. Manufacturers typically measure at 23 feet (7 meters) and at quarter load, but not all of them say so. Honda publishes two figures for the EU2200i, 48 dB(A) at quarter load and 57 dB(A) at rated load. Champion states 53 dBA at 23 feet. Westinghouse quotes 52 dBA as a low point. Yamaha gives a 57 to 65 dBA range. Generac does not publish a figure for the GP2500i.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Decibels Scale</h3>

          <p>
            Decibels are logarithmic. A 10 dB increase sounds roughly twice as loud, and 3 dB is about the smallest change most people notice in ordinary conditions. So the Honda at 48 and the Champion at 53 are noticeably different side by side and much less so across a campsite. The Yamaha at 57 to 65 is a step louder. Any of them is far quieter than an open-frame generator, commonly rated in the high 60s to mid 70s.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Load Matters More Than the Label</h3>

          <p>
            The bigger variable is load. Run any of these near capacity and the engine throttles up to full speed; the EU2200i's own figures rise 9 dB from quarter to rated load. If quiet matters most, size your generator so your typical load is a quarter to half of its rating, and keep economy mode on. For the quietest units across every size class, see our <a href="/best-quiet-generator/">quiet generator guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fuel, Runtime and the One-Gallon Problem
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">About One Gallon of Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            The defining limitation of the class is the tank. At about one gallon, a 2,000-watt inverter will run most of a night at light load and a few hours at heavy load. Honda lists the EU2200i at 8.1 hours at quarter load and 3.2 at rated load. Westinghouse lists the iGen2550 at 11 hours at 25% and 7 at 50%. Generac lists the GP2500i at 8 and 4.5. The WEN's manual gives 5.7 hours at half load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Camping vs Multi-Day Outages</h3>

          <p>
            For camping that is fine: fill it at dinner and it runs past breakfast. For a multi-day outage it means refuelling once or twice a day, and you can't refuel a hot generator safely. Let it cool for a few minutes first, and store gasoline in approved cans with stabilizer, rotated every few months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane Changes the Maths</h3>

          <p>
            Propane changes the maths. The Champion 201323 runs on gasoline or a standard 20-pound propane cylinder, and Champion rates it for up to 34 hours at quarter load on propane, against 11.5 on gasoline. You give up about 10% of running output (1,665 watts on propane), but propane does not go stale or gum up a carburetor, and you probably already own a grill tank. For a generator that may sit unused for a year between outages, that matters more than the lost watts. Our <a href="/best-propane-generator/">propane generator guide</a> covers larger LPG units.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel and Storage Maintenance</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, do the dull maintenance. Run the carburetor dry or add stabilizer before storage, change the oil after the first 20 to 25 hours of break-in (check your manual for the exact interval), and start it monthly. Most small generators that fail to start during an outage fail because of old fuel, not a bad engine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-portability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Weight and Portability: Why 40 to 50 Pounds Is the Point
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Class Exists Because of Weight</h3>

          <p>
            The class exists because of weight. Between 39 pounds (Champion and WEN) and 55 pounds (Yamaha), these are the largest generators most adults can carry with one hand, using the integrated handle, from a garage to a truck bed. Step up to a 3,500 or 4,000-watt inverter and you are at 90 to 105 pounds with wheels and a telescoping handle, which works on a driveway and not on gravel or a campsite.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dry Weight vs Ready-to-Run Weight</h3>

          <p>
            Those numbers are dry weights. A gallon of gasoline adds about six pounds and oil adds another pound or so, so a 47-pound generator is around 54 pounds when it is ready to run. If you have a bad back or will carry it up stairs to a balcony or across a beach, the 16-pound gap between the Champion and the Yamaha is real.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Handle, Feet and Footprint</h3>

          <p>
            Form factor matters as much as weight. Look for a handle centered over the balance point, rubber feet that don't walk on a tailgate, and a fuel gauge you can read in the dark. Measure the space where you will store it, too: these units are about 20 inches long and 18 inches high, and they fit behind a truck seat or in a trailer pass-through compartment, which a 100-pound mid-size will not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-backup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Using One for Home Backup: The Honest Limits
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Will Run</h3>

          <p>
            A 2,000-watt inverter is a legitimate home backup if your expectations match its size. It will keep a refrigerator and a freezer cold, run lights, a router, a TV, phone and laptop chargers, a CPAP, and a gas furnace's blower fan if the furnace is wired to plug in. That covers the most expensive outage risk, which is spoiled food, and the most annoying one, which is no internet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Will Not Run</h3>

          <p>
            It will not run the house. There is no 240V outlet on any unit here, so it cannot power a well pump, a central AC compressor, an electric range or a dryer, and it cannot back-feed both legs of a panel through a transfer switch. If you want to power hard-wired circuits rather than run extension cords, you need at least the 3,500-watt class with a 30-amp outlet; our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">portable generator guide</a> covers that range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Cords and Placement</h3>

          <p>
            Run extension cords properly. Use outdoor-rated 12-gauge cords for anything over about 25 feet, and don't daisy-chain power strips off one outlet. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">extension cord sizing guide</a> has the length-and-load tables. Put the generator at least 20 feet from the house, exhaust pointing away from doors and windows, never in a garage or on a covered porch, even with the door open. CO shut-off sensors on the Champion and Generac are a backstop, not a licence to run it closer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Size Up</h3>

          <p>
            If your outages are long and frequent, two small inverters in parallel or a single mid-size unit will serve you better. If they are a few hours twice a year, a 2,000-watt inverter is the cheapest, quietest and easiest generator to own, and the one you are most likely to actually get out and start.
          </p>
        </div>

