          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 2000 watt inverter generator run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A typical full-size refrigerator runs on 100 to 250 watts and needs about 1,000 to 1,500 watts for a moment when the compressor starts. Every generator in this class starts and runs one easily, and most can carry a chest freezer, lights, a router and phone chargers at the same time, staying under 1,000 running watts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 2000 watt generator run a microwave?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, on its own. A 1,000-watt microwave typically draws 1,400 to 1,600 watts from the outlet, which fits under an 1,800-watt running rating. Run it at the same time as a refrigerator starting up and you may trip the overload. The Generac GP2500i, at 2,200 running watts, gives the most margin for both.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 2000 watt generator run an RV air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally not on a single unit. A 13,500 BTU rooftop AC typically needs 2,500 to 3,500 watts to start. Two 2,000-watt-class units in parallel usually start it, and a soft-start kit on the air conditioner lowers the surge enough that some single units cope. A small 5,000 BTU window unit is usually fine on one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 2000 watt inverter generator run on a tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers list about 8 to 11.5 hours at quarter load from a one-gallon tank, and 3 to 7 hours at half to full load. Honda rates the EU2200i at 8.1 hours at quarter load; Westinghouse rates the iGen2550 at 11. On propane, Champion rates the 201323 at up to 34 hours from a 20-pound tank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Honda EU2200i worth the extra money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you'll use it for years, possibly. It has the lowest published noise figures in the class, a commercial-series engine and strong resale value. It doesn't give you more watts than a Champion or WEN costing half as much. If output per dollar is the priority, the Champion is the better buy; if quiet and longevity matter, the Honda earns its premium.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does parallel capable mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two identical or compatible inverter generators can be linked with a parallel kit so their output combines into one set of outlets. Two 1,800-watt units give about 3,600 running watts. It lets you carry two 40-pound units instead of one 100-pound unit, and run only one when you need less power. Brands generally can't be mixed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I hook a 2000 watt generator to my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not through a standard transfer switch. None of the units here has a 240V outlet, so they cannot feed both legs of a home's panel or power 240V appliances. Use outdoor-rated extension cords to the appliances you need. If you want to power wired circuits, look at a 3,500-watt or larger generator with a 30-amp outlet and a transfer switch.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to the number on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The large number on the box is peak watts. A generator sold as 2,500 watts usually runs at 1,850. Add up your appliances' running watts, then add the single largest starting surge, and compare that to the running rating, not the peak. Most buyers who say their small generator is 'weak' sized it to the peak.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a space heater and expecting the fridge to keep up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500-watt heater on high uses more than 80% of an 1,800-watt generator's continuous output. When the refrigerator compressor tries to start, the overload trips. Use the heater's low setting, around 750 watts, or accept that the heater runs alone and the fridge waits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying two different brands to parallel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Parallel kits only work between the same model or models the manufacturer lists as compatible. A Champion will not parallel with a WEN, and forcing it with an adapter can damage both inverters. If you plan to double up later, buy the first unit with the second in mind.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving gasoline in the carburetor for a year</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Untreated gasoline goes stale in a few months and gums the tiny jets in a small carburetor. That is the most common reason a small generator won't start when the power goes out. Add stabilizer or run it dry with the fuel shut-off, and start it monthly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in the garage with the door open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open garage door does not clear carbon monoxide. Put the generator at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust pointing away from doors and windows. A CO shut-off sensor is a backstop, not permission to move it closer, and a battery CO alarm inside is still essential.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

